  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "What is the smallest result that can be obtained by the following process?\n\n• Choose three different numbers from the set \\(\\{3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17\\}.\\)\n\n• Add two of these numbers.\n\n• Multiply their sum by the third number.",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(15\\)",
    "B": "\\(30\\)",
    "C": "\\(36\\)",
    "D": "\\(50\\)",
    "E": "\\(56\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Separate the possibilities by the number used as the multiplier. For each fixed multiplier, choosing the two smallest other available numbers makes the sum as small as possible.\n\nIf the multiplier is \\(3\\), the smallest result is\n\\[\n3(5+7)=36.\n\\]\nIf the multiplier is \\(5\\), the smallest result is\n\\[\n5(3+7)=50.\n\\]\nIf the multiplier is \\(7\\) or greater, the other two distinct numbers have sum at least \\(3+5\\), so the result is at least\n\\[\n7(3+5)=56.\n\\]\nThese cases cover every allowed multiplier. Since \\(36\\) is attained using the three different numbers \\(3,5,7\\), the smallest result is \\(\\boxed{36}\\), choice C.",
